By Law of Sines on triangle $DFP$, we have $\frac{FP}{\sin(DFP)}=\frac{AP}{ \sin B}$, so $\frac{FP}{\sin(DFP)}=\frac{AP}{\sin B}$, so $\frac{FP}{\sin(DFP)}=\frac{BQ}{ \sin D}$.
Thus, $\tan B_Y = \tan D_X$.
By the Law of Sines on triangle $DXP$, we have $\frac{XP}{\sin DXP}=\frac{PX}{\sin DPX}$, so $\frac{XP}{\sin DXP}=\frac{2\Pi}{\sin DPX}$.
Since $\angle XYP=\frac{\pi}{2}-B_A-\theta$ and $\angle XYP = \frac{\pi}{2}+\alpha$, we can deduce that $DPQ=2\alpha$ and $QYP=\theta$.
Thus, $\tan 2\alpha = \tan 2\theta$.
